.TH CHTTPCLI_SET_ENGINE_MEM_MGMT_PROCS 3 "2026-07-14" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpcli_set_engine_mem_mgmt_procs \- set custom memory management for chttpclient's async-engine allocations
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <chttpclient.h>

ccol_retval_t chttpcli_set_engine_mem_mgmt_procs(ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mp);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Redirects every allocation made by the event_loop reactor shared by
every chttpcli instance's Tier 2/3 work in this process (the
registration table, per-connection dispatch state, the DNS/connect
worker pool, and so on) to the malloc/free/calloc/realloc supplied in
mp, exactly like the memory management procs accepted by every other
module in this library.  Passing NULL reverts to the default allocator.

This is independent of the allocator each individual chttpcli instance
uses for its own requests/connections (configured via
create_chttpclient_mp, following the usual _mp convention); this
function only affects the one shared reactor's own construction.

May only be called before the reactor has ever started in this process
(i.e. before the first chttpclient_do_async/_streaming call anywhere),
or after the engine has fully stopped (every chttpcli async user has
released its reference and the automatic teardown has completed).
Unlike chttpsvr_set_engine_mem_mgmt_procs's typically process-lifetime
reactor, this engine starts and stops on its own as Tier 2/3 usage
comes and goes, so this window recurs naturally rather than being a
one-shot process-startup opportunity.

If mp is non-NULL, all four function pointers (malloc, free, calloc,
realloc) must be set.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success on success.
ccol_invalid_args if mp is non-NULL but has a NULL function pointer.
ccol_not_permitted if the engine is currently running; wait for it to
    fully stop first.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpcli_set_engine_logger (3),
.BR chttpclient_do_async (3),
.BR chttpclient_do_pooled (3),
.BR chttpsvr_set_engine_mem_mgmt_procs (3)
